<TopicPage slug="subjects/occupational-therapy/foundational/occupation-the-core-construct">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The word "occupation" misleads every newcomer. In occupational therapy it does not mean a
    job — it means <strong>everything people do to occupy themselves: the self-care they must
    do, the productivity they choose, the leisure they love</strong>. Dressing, cooking,
    parenting, commuting, gaming, praying, working. The founding insight of the profession is
    that these ordinary activities are not just goals of health care but active ingredients in
    it: doing shapes health, identity and capability. Where medicine repairs the mechanism, OT
    restores the person's ability to do what matters to them — and when the mechanism cannot be
    fully repaired, it redesigns the task, the tool or the environment instead. Occupation is
    the core construct because it is simultaneously the <em>means</em>, the <em>end</em> and the
    <em>measure</em> of the therapy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="activity analysis: one cup of tea, dissected into its demand structure" widget="equationStep" config={teaCfg} />

  <h2>What occupation means</h2>
  <p>
    Occupations are usually grouped as <strong>activities of daily living</strong> (bathing,
    dressing, feeding), <strong>instrumental ADLs</strong> (cooking, finance, transport,
    medication management), <strong>work and education</strong>, <strong>play, leisure and
    social participation</strong>, and rest and sleep. Two properties make an activity an
    occupation rather than a task: it is <em>personally meaningful</em>, and it occupies a place
    in a person's roles and routines — "making breakfast" is also "being a mother before the
    school run". The flip side of the construct is occupational deprivation: refugees, prisoners
    and the institutionalised show what happens to health when the opportunity to do meaningful
    occupation is removed. Occupational performance — the actual doing of the occupation in
    context — is the profession's outcome measure.
  </p>

  <h2>Models that organise it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MOHO — the Model of Human Occupation.</strong> Kielhofner's systems model explains doing through <em>volition</em> (values, interests, sense of capacity), <em>habituation</em> (habits and roles) and <em>performance capacity</em> (body and mind), all interacting with a physical and social environment. Assessment and therapy follow: why does this person <em>choose</em> not to do, and what would rebuild the choice?</li>
    <li><strong>CMOP-E.</strong> The Canadian model draws occupation at the centre of the person (affective, cognitive, physical domains around a core of spirituality), nested in cultural, institutional, physical and social environments. Its transverse strip marks occupational performance and engagement — the meeting of person, occupation and world.</li>
    <li><strong>Models in use.</strong> Models are lenses, not doctrines: they tell the therapist what to observe (MOHO's volition loss; CMOP-E's environment mismatch), guide assessment choice, and keep the intervention occupation-centred rather than impairment-obsessed.</li>
  </ul>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a century of occupation-centred thinking — scrub from the 1917 founding to CMOP-E" widget="timeline" config={otTimeline} />

  <h2>Activity analysis</h2>
  <p>
    The fig. 1 walk-through of making tea is the profession's signature skill: taking any
    occupation apart into objects, sequence, body functions, and social–safety demands, then
    <strong>grading</strong> it (harder: bigger kettle, thinner handle, standing) or
    <strong>adapting</strong> it (jar opener, seated version, reordered steps) to match the
    client's capacities. Activity analysis is what lets the same therapist treat a child with
    cerebral palsy, a worker with a back injury and an elder with dementia — by choosing and
    tuning occupations, not exercises in the abstract. It is also the bridge between the models
    and the clinic: CMOP-E says look at environment and person together; the analysis says
    exactly which environment factor and which body function this task, for this person, will
    stress.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Willard &amp; Spackman's Occupational Therapy (13th ed.); Kielhofner, Model of Human Occupation: Theory and Application; CAOT, Enabling Occupation II (2007); AOTA, Occupational Therapy Practice Framework.</p>
